# CM Computation cleanup — completed 1 October 2026

`CM Computation` now has exactly two unpacked folders:

1. [Current_Compiler_Study](<../CM Computation/Current_Compiler_Study/README_BUNDLE.md>) — current **Operator-Level Boolean Computation with Correspondence Matrices** manuscript and the later synthetic/natural study evidence. The [PDF](<../CM Computation/Current_Compiler_Study/02_CONTRACT_REVISION_20260926/Operator_Level_CM_Compiler_Revised_Draft.pdf>) and [TeX](<../CM Computation/Current_Compiler_Study/02_CONTRACT_REVISION_20260926/Operator_Level_CM_Compiler_Revised_Draft.tex>) are the paper entry points.
2. [Current_Compiler_Audit_Report](<../CM Computation/Current_Compiler_Audit_Report/README_AUDIT_BUNDLE.md>) — the distinct publication-readiness audit report and its build, evidence, and verification files. Its [PDF](<../CM Computation/Current_Compiler_Audit_Report/report/CM_Publication_Readiness_Audit_2026-09-26.pdf>) is the report entry point. The 22 files were extracted safely and verified byte-for-byte against the unchanged audit ZIP.

The 19 prior loose folders shown in the cleanup plan, comprising 2,270 files, were removed after their files were matched by SHA-256 to retained locations. The September 22 revision ZIP, two original intermediate revision ZIPs, five original root ZIPs, the preserved former-root ZIP, a new history ZIP, a new viability-review ZIP, and a supplemental preservation ZIP remain. The separate CM Structural Computation Draft 3 has its own sibling paper home; the reference ZIP here remains, but its extracted duplicate is gone. The MIND and the sibling SC home were not changed by this cleanup.

## Preservation records

- [Pre-cleanup source inventory](CM_COMPUTATION_PRECLEANUP_SOURCE_2026-10-01.jsonl): original path, byte count and SHA-256 for all 2,284 files then present under `CM Computation`.
- [Relocation map](CM_COMPUTATION_RELOCATION_MAP_2026-10-01.jsonl): a verified current-file or `ZIP!member` location for every one of those 2,284 files; zero mismatches after removal.
- [Archive checks](CM_COMPUTATION_ARCHIVE_CHECKS_2026-10-01.json): size, SHA-256 and CRC result for 11 ZIPs now at `CM Computation` root plus the preserved former-root ZIP.
- [Post-cleanup source inventory](CM_COMPUTATION_POSTCLEANUP_SOURCE_2026-10-01.jsonl): final paths, sizes and hashes, including both readable folders.
- [Earlier coverage analysis](CM_COMPUTATION_ZIP_COVERAGE_2026-10-01.json) and [cleanup plan](CM_COMPUTATION_ONE_FOLDER_PLAN_2026-10-01.md) document the decision. The historical `SOURCE_MANIFEST_SHA256.csv` remains unchanged; its former paths are interpreted through the relocation map.

`CM_Computation_Loose_Only_2026-10-01.zip` is a supplemental preservation snapshot, not a paper version. It contains 649 then-unmatched loose files, mainly generated bytecode, plus five already-preserved ZIPs. It is larger and more redundant than intended, but its bytes and member CRCs are verified. All original and newly created ZIPs were kept. The current study's 342 non-cache paths match the frozen study ZIP; five navigation Markdown files were updated in the readable copy to point to the new layout, and their original bytes remain in the frozen ZIP.

## Checks and remaining limits

- Every relocation target was reopened and SHA-256 checked after removal: **2,284/2,284 match**.
- All 12 checked ZIPs retained their recorded size and SHA-256 and passed a member CRC test. The study ZIP's original `.sha256` receipt still matches.
- The updated compiler publication recipe was tested in an isolated temporary output. Its **359 included members have the same paths and SHA-256 hashes** as the existing September 30 publication ZIP; no release output was overwritten. The full release recipe depends on other external/project sources and was not rerun end-to-end.
- A local Markdown-link check of the collection guides, both current folders, root index and relevant cross-reference files found **zero broken local links**. Historical partition reports still quote former paths as records of their original inventories; use the relocation map for those paths.
- Git now records numerous deletions of old tracked loose paths and new untracked readable folders. The repository ignores `*.zip`, so the preserved ZIPs are present on disk but do not appear as new Git files. No files were staged, committed or pushed.

